}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy-gated progression system: the player has a limited amount of energy, cannot progress after it is depleted, and must wait for a recovery timer. This is an in-game timer that imposes an arbitrary wait before progression can resume, matching Playing by Appointment. The review does not say that energy can be purchased, so Pay to Progress is not supported. The requests for console play, a storyline, bigger maps, and customization are hypothetical additions and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers.",
      "rationale": "Progress is halted when energy is depleted and the player must wait for the game's recovery timer before continuing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchasing Poké Balls, using them for capture attempts, and having Pokémon repeatedly break out so the consumable items are depleted and more must be purchased. The capture outcome is chance-based, and real-money purchases supply repeated attempts for the possibility of obtaining a Pokémon. The later edit disputes authorship and the claim about speaking with other players, but it does not identify a bug or technical fault in the capture mechanic, so the described purchase-and-chance pattern remains codable.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Loot Boxes / Gambling definition",
      "rationale": "The player purchases consumable Poké Balls to make repeated chance-based capture attempts, with the possibility of receiving a Pokémon as the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes the game forcing the player to grant access to their contact list, apparently as a condition associated with notifications. It does not explicitly say that the game actually sends messages or invitations to those contacts, so the supported mechanic is the game's attempt to obtain access to the player's contact list. The statement that it is a spam app is an evaluation and does not add another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "give you permission to my contact list",
      "rule_applied": "indicator: Game uses access to the player’s contact list / social graph",
      "rationale": "The game is described as requiring access to the player's contact list, which is an explicit contact-list access behavior covered by this label."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress being blocked by difficult levels and tasks that are effectively impossible without purchasing boosters. The explicit payment gate supports Pay to Progress. The statement that passing hard levels takes a week indicates prolonged difficulty, but it does not explicitly describe repetitive or monotonous tasks, so it is not enough for Grinding. No other listed mechanic is clearly present.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review states that completing in-game tasks is effectively contingent on buying boosters, making payment necessary for progress."
    }
  ],
