{
  "analysis": "The review describes monetized progression: the reviewer says progress without paying is effectively unavailable, and that keeping up with the game's changing competitive meta requires buying the newest bot rather than earning it. The newest bot purchase also grants a gameplay advantage, supporting pay-to-progress through the codebook's combined pay-to-skip/pay-to-win definition. The game repeatedly presents in-app purchase offers on login before play, but the review does not describe a specific purchase-default, accidental-purchase, or attention-directing UI behavior, so Easy to Purchase and UI Misdirection are not assigned. It also states that free-to-play rewards are obtained through ads, which describes incentivized advertising. I am not assigning Competition or FOMO merely from the game's multiplayer context or the phrase 'keep up,' because the review does not explicitly describe competing players or fear of missing rewards.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Pay to Progress definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes needing to purchase the newest bot with real money to maintain gameplay-relevant progress and competitive viability."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rule_applied": "definition; incentivized to view advertisements through the promise of rewards",
      "rationale": "The span links free-to-play rewards to watching advertisements, describing rewarded or incentivized ad viewing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a card-collection mechanic in which the player tries to complete a set and earn its completion reward. Near the end of the collection period, card packs stop providing needed new cards, leaving the player with an incomplete set despite having collected 125 cards. The expiring collection creates a risk of missing the reward, which pressures completion before the event ends. The text does not explicitly describe payment, gambling, ads, daily login requirements, or a deliberate scarcity tactic tied to purchases.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "after one is almost close to completing a set, new cards don't appear again",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The mechanic centers on acquiring all cards needed to complete a collection set."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "you will miss the reward for the set completion",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The expiring collection creates pressure through the possibility of missing an important completion reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "after collecting 125 cards from the beginning of the collection you will miss the reward for the set completion",
      "rule_applied": "Endowed Progress",
      "rationale": "The player has accumulated substantial progress toward a partially completed goal, making the lost completion reward and prior investment salient."
